 ial interventions\, and sculpture\, with a focus on materials and sites i
 n transition.</p><p class="p1">  Architecture becomes an entry-point to q
 uestions of land use\, ownership and access\, bodily experience and perce
 ption\, and timeframes ranging from the human to the geological. Lightbou
 nd's talk will tease out loose threads in seemingly fixed systems of spat
 ial organization\, inviting viewers to consider and participate in the co
 ntinual (re)construction of meaning in urban environments.</p><p class="p
 1">  Frances Lightbound holds a B.A. from Glasgow School of Art\, Scotlan
 d\, and an M.F.A. from the School of the Art Institute of Chicago. Recent
  solo exhibitions include Louise Hopkins Center for the Arts (Lubbock\, T
 X)\, RULE Gallery (Marfa\, TX)\, and the John David Mooney Foundation (Ch
 icago\, IL)\; her work has been exhibited internationally at venues in Lo
 ndon\, Glasgow and Berlin. Lightbound is a Lecturer at School of the Art 
 Institute of Chicago in the departments of Print media and Photography.</
 p><p class="p1"><em>The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture and 
 Exhibition Series brings prominent working artists and architects to Colu
 mbus. Lectures take place in the auditorium and exhibitions in the galler
 y\, both located within The Republic Building\, 333 Second Street in Colu
 mbus. The gallery is open Monday through Friday from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All
  events are free and open to the public.</em></p>
