SUMMARY:David Bamber: The Invisible Hand | Lecture
DESCRIPTION:Widely recognized for his decades-long creative relationship 
 with Tom Ford and his prominent role within major global fashion houses. 
 His career spans high-profile positions in luxury womenswear\, creative d
 irection\, film\, theater\, and education.  After graduating with first-c
 lass honors in Fashion and Textiles from Gloucestershire College of Art a
 nd Design\, Bamber spent his early career honing his technical skills in 
 Christian Dior's tailoring and dressmaking workrooms and developing his n
 ow renowned eye for detail. Subsequently\, Bamber worked as a costume des
 igner for production teams both in the U.K. and internationally and as se
 nior designer with Calvin Klein.  Within five years\, Bamber joined Gucci
  to rebuild the then-struggling brand. Working under Tom Ford\, Bamber wa
 s a major contributor to the success of the brand and was seconded to bot
 h Stella McCartney and Yves Saint Laurent. During his tenure at Gucci\, s
 ales increased from 230 million dollars in 1994 to almost 3 billion dolla
 rs in 2003\, making Gucci one of the most profitable brands in the world.
   Since leaving Gucci in 2006\, Bamber has consulted across fashion\, fil
 m\, theater\, and music\, and worked extensively with Tom Ford until 2023
 .  In this talk\, Bamber will share the key pillars of a career that has 
 shaped the history of fashion design and luxury brands: trust\, partnersh
 ip\, education\, discipline\, craftmanship\, taste\, and legacy.The Bill 
 Blass Speaker Series was established in 2002 with a bequest from fashion 
 designer Bill Blass\, a native Hoosier and friend of Indiana University. 
 The Bill Blass Speaker Series is presented and hosted by the Eskenazi Sch
 ool of Art\, Architecture + Design and the College of Arts and Sciences.
