SUMMARY:Matthew Mindrup: Architecture Forgets\, An Alternative View | Lec
 ture
DESCRIPTION:Buildings and cities\, Mindrup argues\, endure not by remaini
 ng unchanged but by repeatedly relinquishing what they once were.  Mindru
 p is an architect\, scholar\, and associate professor of architecture at 
 the University of Sydney\, where he directed the multidisciplinary Bachel
 or of Architecture and Environments from 2019 to 2025. He is a senior fel
 low of the Higher Education Academy and has taught architecture at instit
 utions in the United States\, Switzerland\, Canada\, and Australia.  His 
 forthcoming book\, "Architecture Forgets: The Art and Tactics of Transfor
 mation" (MIT Press\, 2026)\, turns from how architecture comes into being
  to how it changes over time.The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lec
 ture and Exhibition Series brings prominent working artists and architect
 s to Columbus. Lectures take place in the auditorium and exhibitions in t
 he gallery\, both located within The Republic Building\, 333 Second Stree
 t in Columbus. The gallery is open Monday through Friday from 9 a.m. to 5
  p.m. All events are free and open to the public.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1">  Buildings and cities\, Mindr
 up argues\, endure not by remaining unchanged but by repeatedly relinquis
 hing what they once were.</p><p class="p1">  Mindrup is an architect\, sc
 holar\, and associate professor of architecture at the University of Sydn
 ey\, where he directed the multidisciplinary Bachelor of Architecture and
  Environments from 2019 to 2025. He is a senior fellow of the Higher Educ
 ation Academy and has taught architecture at institutions in the United S
 tates\, Switzerland\, Canada\, and Australia.</p><p class="p1">  His fort
 hcoming book\, "Architecture Forgets: The Art and Tactics of Transformati
 on" (MIT Press\, 2026)\, turns from how architecture comes into being to 
 how it changes over time.</p><p class="p1"><em>The J. Irwin Miller Archit
 ecture Program Lecture and Exhibition Series brings prominent working art
 ists and architects to Columbus. Lectures take place in the auditorium an
 d exhibitions in the gallery\, both located within The Republic Building\
 , 333 Second Street in Columbus. The gallery is open Monday through Frida
 y from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All events are free and open to the public.</em><
 /p>
